# Production Planner Agent - Cursor Rules

Purpose: keep changes production-safe, consistent, and easy to review across the FastAPI backend and React frontend.

## 1) Architecture and boundaries
- Respect current project structure:
  - Frontend: `frontend/src/pages`, `frontend/src/components`, `frontend/src/hooks`, `frontend/src/store`, `frontend/src/lib`, `frontend/src/types`.
  - Backend: `backend/app/api`, `backend/app/agent`, `backend/app/models`, `backend/app/schemas`, `backend/app/core`, `backend/app/services`.
- Keep business logic out of UI components and FastAPI route handlers.
- Route handlers should orchestrate, not implement core logic.

## 2) Type safety and contracts
- Use strict TypeScript and explicit Python typing.
- Avoid `any` unless there is a documented reason.
- Preserve API contracts between backend schemas and frontend types.
- Validate inbound backend payloads with schema models before processing.

## 3) Data and API access patterns
- Frontend HTTP calls should go through shared API client and hooks (`frontend/src/lib/axios.ts` + domain hooks).
- Do not add ad-hoc API calls directly in presentational UI components.
- Backend third-party AI calls must stay centralized under `backend/app/agent`.

## 4) Security and secrets
- Never hardcode secrets, tokens, or credentials.
- Use environment variables (`GROQ_API_KEY`, `DATABASE_URL`, `JWT_SECRET_KEY`, etc.).
- Keep `.env` out of commits; maintain `.env.example` with safe placeholders only.

## 5) UI/UX consistency
- Follow existing dark + accent design system (ProdIQ style) and current responsive behavior.
- Reuse shared components before introducing new ones.
- Preserve accessibility basics: semantic elements, labels, and button/link intent.

## 6) Benchmark and scoring safety
- Treat benchmark logic as objective and deterministic by default.
- Do not silently alter scoring scales, caps, or layer weights unless explicitly requested.
- If changing benchmark behavior, document the rationale clearly in code and README.

## 7) Docker and environment compatibility
- Keep Docker Compose flows first-class for local setup.
- Avoid machine-specific assumptions in code paths and docs.
- Ensure updates remain compatible with Windows + Docker Desktop workflows.

## 8) Quality checks before completion
- For frontend changes: run build/lint checks when possible.
- For backend changes: run compile/lint/test checks when possible.
- If a check cannot be run, state what remains and how to run it.

## 9) Editing discipline
- Make minimal, focused diffs.
- Do not refactor unrelated areas during feature/bug tasks.
- Do not remove or overwrite user content unless explicitly requested.
- Update docs (`README.md`) when behavior, setup, or workflows change.
